  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 1213 AM MDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nFriday and Saturday, the area is forecast to be stuck in the middle \nof multiple features with an upper trough over the Western United \nStates and Northern Plains, while a hurricane pushes into the Ohio \nRiver Valley. In spite of all these features in the United States, \nthis should leave the area in a relatively stagnant pattern with a \nsurface low hanging around the High and Northern Plains that extends \ninto the area. This should keep southerly low level flow and dry air \nplace over the area, allowing temperatures to warm into the mid 80s \nto lower 90s. The dry air and warm temperatures are forecast to have \nrelatively humidity lower into the teens and 20s during the \nafternoon, with the lowest values for counties along the Colorado \nborder. With the pressure gradient over the area keeping winds \naround 15-20 mph and gusts around 30 mph, there is increasing \nconcern for at least a few hours of critical fire weather \nconditions. But with ensemble spread charts showing most of the \nfeatures could either shift or become more broad, don't have the \nconfidence to issue any products yet as conditions could still \nchange a bit. One of the things I am more confident in is the lack \nof precipitation. While we have features near/over the area, the air \nwill likely be too dry for anything to form, keeping our precip \nchances under 5%.\n\nSunday and early next week remain a mess with two northern upper \ntroughs possible along the the upper low over the Western United \nStates potentially moving near the area. For now, it is likely that \nwe will have westerly/southwesterly flow with all these features \nthat should bring some mid to high level moisture into the area. The \nincreased cloud cover and influence of the upper troughs trying to \npush the surface low east and allow for cold air advection should \nlead to cooler temperatures for that start of the week. For now, the \nforecast is that temperatures lower each day by about 5 degrees with \nhighs forecast to be in the mid 60s be mid-week. With the \nuncertainty in how strong each feature will be and when it will \nadvance, some ensemble solutions suggest much colder air would be \npossible. Even so, the chance for freezing temperatures is only \naround 5-10%. This has also severely lower the snow chances hinted \nat by prior runs in guidance. What hasn't lowered is the chance for \nprecipitation with the influx of mid and upper level moisture. While \nunlikely to be widespread or lead to decent accumulations due to dry \nnear surface air, Mon-Wed has a 30-50% chance of rain each day.",
